  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 PM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nAfternoon water vapor imagery and RAP analysis show mid-upper level \nlows spinning over the Canadian Prairie and over Arizona, with the \nridge axis directed right over the Great Lakes. At the surface, low \npressure is moving into northern Ontario, with a cold front \nextending southward through MN, and a surface high sprawled out over \nthe Northeast. This is leading to southerly flow into the UP, where \ntemperatures are climbing into the low to mid 70s (west) and the low \nto mid 60s (east). Otherwise, southerly winds are turning gusty \ncourtesy of the tight pressure gradient over the area; gusts to 20-\n30mph are becoming common, but wouldn't be shocked by some higher \ngusts in excess of 30 mph across the north-central UP and in the \ndownsloping areas near Lake Superior. \n\nScattered rain showers ahead of the aforementioned cold front are \nalready reaching the MN Arrowhead, and finally reach the western UP \ncloser to 00Z as the cold front works eastward. Showers continue to \nspread west-to-east across the UP tonight into early Sunday as the \nfront moves through, becoming more isolated in the eastern UP Sunday \nmorning as drier air moves in behind the front. Rain totals of 0.1in \nor less are expected area-wide. Westerly winds behind the cold front \nbring cooler, near-normal temps peaking in the 50s to low 60s \nSunday, coldest northwest and warmest in the south-central UP. Some \nbreaks in the clouds in the afternoon hours as well as much drier \nTds in the 20s in the west support RH falling into the mid 20 to mid \n30s interior west. Higher minimum RH in the upper 30s to mid 60s is \nexpected in the Keweenaw and east. Thankfully, winds gusts remain \nunder 15 mph where RH is lowest with less than a 15% chance of gusts \nabove 20 mph with RH below 30% anywhere in the UP, limiting fire \nweather concerns.\n\nRidging begins to recover Monday into Tuesday, though an embedded \nshortwave lifts north over the Dakotas Monday night through Tuesday, \nsupporting a weak low to the west which may graze the western UP \nwith some rain showers Monday night through Tuesday afternoon. This \nquickly is followed up by a more vigorous shortwave moving over the \nsame area, sending another sfc low to the west of the CWA, keeping \nshower chances in the forecast through Wednesday night. \nDiscrepancies remain on exact track, but best rain chances (40-70%) \nare over the west during both events. Guidance quickly begins to \nstruggle after Thursday, but a more active period is anticipated to \ncontinue into early November captured by the CPC's 6-10 \nprecipitation outlook of slightly above normal precip.\n\nOtherwise, temps return near normal in the wake of the cold front on \nSunday peaking in the 40s to low 50s Monday and Tuesday. Lows Sunday \nnight and monday night are expected in the upper 20s to low 40s. \nSoutherly flow returning for Wednesday and Thursday warms temps into \nthe 50s to low 60s with lows in the mid 30s and mid 40s.",
